steps.body:
An `if` alone runs its block or skips it. Add `else:` to give the program a second path. The `else` has no condition of its own: it runs exactly when the `if` condition is false, so **exactly one of the two blocks runs**, never both and never neither.

```python
temp = 31
if temp > 25:
    print("Hot")
else:
    print("Not hot")
```

`else` lines up with its `if` and ends with a colon. Writing a condition after it, such as `else temp <= 25:`, is a SyntaxError.

A branch is an ordinary block, so it can hold another `if`. That is a **nested if**: it is only checked when the outer branch runs. Indentation decides which `if` an `else` belongs to:

```python harness
for temp in [31, 40]:
    {{code}}
```

```python
if temp > 25:
    if temp > 35:
        print("Very hot")
    else:
        print("Warm")
```

This `else` lines up with the inner `if`, so it runs when `temp` is above 25 but not above 35. Move it to the left edge and it pairs with the outer `if` instead.

Keep nesting shallow. Two levels is easy to read. Five is not.

